# Lines to merge into ~/.config/speech-dispatcher/speechd.conf — NOT a
# complete file. See the README's "Creating the user speechd.conf safely"
# step for why this must be merged, not dropped in whole, and why you
# should not run `spd-conf -u` after piper.conf already exists.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# GOTCHA: speech-dispatcher auto-detects and silently loads whichever
# modules it finds working when NO explicit AddModule line is present
# anywhere in speechd.conf. The moment you add ONE explicit AddModule
# line (for piper), that autodetection turns off entirely and ONLY the
# modules you listed explicitly will load. If you were relying on
# espeak-ng (or anything else) working before, and you only add the
# piper line below, you will SILENTLY LOSE every other voice the user
# had. Always add explicit AddModule lines for every module you want,
# not just the new one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Keep (uncomment, or add) whatever the target already had working.
# espeak-ng ships with speech-dispatcher's own package on every distro
# tested so far (Ubuntu/Zorin, and presumably Fedora/Nobara); the dummy
# fallback module is likewise always present and should always stay.
AddModule "espeak-ng" "sd_espeak-ng" "espeak-ng.conf"
AddModule "dummy"     "sd_dummy"    ""

# The line this runbook is actually about:
AddModule "piper" "sd_generic" "piper.conf"

# GOTCHA: openjtalk (Japanese) was present on the machine this was built
# on, so it got added here too — but its binary is an OPTIONAL package
# (speech-dispatcher-openjtalk or similar) that may not exist on a fresh
# install. Before adding this line, check:
#   ls /usr/lib/speech-dispatcher-modules/ | grep openjtalk
# If it's not there, DO NOT add this line — a missing binary for an
# explicitly-AddModule'd entry can be worse than a module that merely
# fails to find voices (it may refuse to start at all), and it isn't
# needed for Learn Anywhere's languages anyway.
# AddModule "openjtalk" "sd_openjtalk" "openjtalk.conf"

# This is what actually fixes BOTH of the following, with zero changes
# to Learn Anywhere itself:
#  - which voice "Automatic" picks for a language Piper also covers
#  - which module's voices Options ▸ Speech... lists FIRST for every
#    language (the app's listOutputModules() in
#    packages/ui_kit/lib/src/speech/speech_dispatcher_voices.dart always
#    puts whatever DefaultModule names at the top of the list; eSpeak
#    NG's ~13,000 voice/language-variant entries will otherwise bury
#    Piper's handful at the bottom of every dropdown)
DefaultModule piper
